<div class="shead"><h2>Government records should not look like a fax from 1997.</h2></div>
<div class="about">
  <p class="lede">Tech and geopolitics sounds like a story about AI, cyber espionage, and backroom deals for GPUs in the Situation Room. Mostly, it is a story about paperwork.</p>

  <p>The decisions that shape what technology you can build, buy, and sell — and to whom — are buried in orders, agreements, lists, and footnotes, filed under docket numbers in government systems that predate SQL. Technically, they are on the internet. In practice, they are scanned PDFs you cannot search and cannot read on your phone.</p>

  <h3>What NatSec Noir is</h3>
  <p>NatSec Noir is a public reading room for the regulatory record of tech and geopolitics, from a US point of view. The collection includes:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>CFIUS presidential orders</strong> under Section 721 of the Defense Production Act, which block or unwind deals</li>
    <li><strong>The FCC Covered List</strong> and the equipment-authorization orders behind it</li>
    <li><strong>Team Telecom mitigation agreements</strong> that foreign-owned carriers sign to keep their licenses</li>
    <li><strong>The Commerce Entity List</strong> and <strong>ICTS actions</strong> against connected devices and software</li>
    <li><strong>The Pentagon’s Section 1260H list</strong> of Chinese military companies</li>
    <li><strong>The lawsuits</strong> challenging all of the above</li>
  </ul>
  <p>We download each record, run text recognition on the scans, publish it as a real web page, and add a plain-English summary of what it does. Every record links back to its source. Only the source is authoritative.</p>

  <h3>Public records belong to the public, not to the insiders who can pay for them.</h3>
  <p>The stakes are concrete. The Covered List decides which cameras, routers, and radios can be sold in this country. The Entity List decides whether an American supplier can ship a chip to a given company. A Team Telecom agreement can dictate where a carrier stores Americans’ call records and who may touch its network. These are among the most consequential tech-policy decisions Washington makes, and they are about as accessible as a WordPerfect file on a floppy disk.</p>

  <p>If you build products in San Francisco, Taipei, or Singapore, DC may feel like somebody else’s problem. It is not. Washington does not need your attention to make decisions about your company.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Raising money from overseas?</strong> CFIUS may care about your cap table before your board does.</li>
    <li><strong>Building hardware?</strong> Your bill of materials touches the Entity List and the Covered List.</li>
    <li><strong>Selling connected devices or apps?</strong> ICTS rules can decide whether your product ships at all.</li>
    <li><strong>Running an AI or cloud company?</strong> Export controls on chips and models are now part of your roadmap.</li>
    <li><strong>In-house counsel?</strong> You get the 6 p.m. email asking, “Are we on any of these lists?”</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Access should not be the price of admission</h3>
  <p>Today, most real knowledge of these rules lives in two places: the heads of the officials who wrote them, and the law firms that hire those officials when they leave government.</p>

  <p>Tech policy should not be shaped only by who gets into the right room. Readable public records are where a fairer fight starts. Turning scanned PDFs into searchable, quotable, linkable text is the least glamorous work on the internet. It is also some of the most important.</p>

  <h3>Laid side by side, the records tell a story</h3>
  <p>The <a href="/analyses/">Analyses</a> section lines up records that live in separate dockets: CFIUS orders, Team Telecom agreements, FCC conditional approvals, the restricted-entity lists. Side by side, patterns appear — which conditions recur, which agencies ask for what, and who keeps landing on which list. No single filing shows you that. The pattern should be visible to the public, not only to the few dozen lobbyists who trade it over lunch.</p>

  <h3>The technology behind it</h3>
  <p>The reading room is the front end. Behind it, the same records power a set of experiments in where legal AI is heading.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Clean text first.</strong> PyMuPDF extracts text from each document. Pages it cannot read go to Tesseract for OCR.</li>
    <li><strong>Search that finds the right passage.</strong> Records are chunked, tagged, and embedded with Voyage 4 nano, running locally on a mini PC. Hybrid search combines BM25 keyword matching with semantic retrieval.</li>
    <li><strong>A fast, simple database.</strong> Documents and their LLM-generated summaries live in SQLite, which is all a project at this scale needs.</li>
    <li><strong>Chat with citations.</strong> In development: a chat interface that answers in plain language and points to the exact passage it relied on.</li>
    <li><strong>A knowledge graph</strong> that maps entities, sources, and proceedings, so every answer stays tied to its record — including proceedings that go to a federal court of appeals and come back to the agency on remand.</li>
    <li><strong>LLM-maintained wikis.</strong> A test of whether an LLM can keep entity and proceeding pages current without losing the audit trail. The hard part is not writing the page. It is letting lawyers verify it against the source.</li>
    <li><strong>Evaluation sets</strong> to measure reliability, which remains the hardest problem.</li>
    <li><strong>Access for other AI agents.</strong> Next: an MCP server and open knowledge formats, so other agents can query the collection directly.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>One rule governs all of it: <strong>the source document wins.</strong></p>

  <p>That rule matters well beyond this site. You cannot build trustworthy legal AI on scanned images nobody can parse. Clean, cited, structured public records come first. The government holds mountains of them in the worst possible format. NatSec Noir is a working demonstration of what the raw material should look like.</p>

  <h3>Why we are optimistic</h3>
  <p>This is fixable, and cheaply. Text recognition is cheap. Good search is open source. AI can now read a forty-page order and explain it in plain English, as long as it shows its sources. One editor with the right tools can do what the agencies have not.</p>

  <p>Posting a PDF was supposed to be where public access started. Somewhere along the way, that was where it ended. NatSec Noir picks up from there, one record at a time.</p>

  <h3>A note on authority</h3>
  <p>Records here are public documents. Summaries are machine-generated readings of them and are not authoritative. Nothing on this site is legal advice. When accuracy matters, cite the source document, not the summary.</p>

